In comp.unix.osf.misc, <ydzhang@iastate.edu> writes:
>When I registered to a DEC UNIX alpha machine, I incidently hitted
>register twince. As a result, there are two entries for this product
>were contained in the   License Database file,  for example, there are
>two entries for FORTRAN,
>Product                   Status                     Users: Total
>Active

>FORTRAN-O                 active                            unlimited
>FORTRAN-O                 active, multiple                  unlimited

>I tried to delete it,
> by lmf delete FORTRAN-O, it gives me following error:
>Information provided was ambiguous; multiple licenses were found

>simply this product can not be removed, can someone offer help,

I expect you have two Product Authorization Keys (PAKs): one expiring
some time after the other, though neither has expired yet.

>I tried to delete it,
> by lmf delete FORTRAN-O, it gives me following error:
>Information provided was ambiguous; multiple licenses were found

As you'll see from "man lmf", each PAK has three identifying parts:
product, producer, and authorization number.  Run the following
command.

	# lmf list full for fortran-o
             Product Name: FORTRAN-O
                 Producer: DEC
                   Issuer: DEC
     Authorization Number: CAMPUSWIDE-4000
     Key Termination Date: 30-MAR-2001
     [[ more info, but I don't believe it's relevant ]]

This will provide you with produce and authorization number for each
PAK.  Look at the Key Termination Dates.  That should identify the PAK
you want to remove.

	lmf delete fortran-o dec campuswide-4000

Paul Fardy
